WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.007
Hi, and welcome.

00:00:01.007 --> 00:00:06.479
Jordan is studying for a history quiz and keeps mixing up two causes of a major event.

00:00:06.479 --> 00:00:12.204
A chat tool could give a long summary, but Jordan needs practice telling the causes apart.

00:00:12.204 --> 00:00:20.256
As you watch, keep one question in mind: The best study prompt sets up useful practice, then gives you room to do the learning.

00:00:20.256 --> 00:00:23.087
You do not need to memorize a list of terms.

00:00:23.087 --> 00:00:30.384
Try to notice what the people in the story know, what they still need to check, and what choice they can make next.

00:00:30.934 --> 00:00:35.235
A helpful prompt is a little like giving directions to a study partner.

00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:41.777
Say where you are stuck, what kind of help you want, and when you would rather try before seeing an answer.

00:00:41.777 --> 00:00:45.169
This comparison is a way to think, not a perfect match.

00:00:45.169 --> 00:00:51.772
Real AI systems can work in different ways, so it helps to ask what this particular tool was designed to do.

00:00:51.772 --> 00:00:55.286
Build prompts with a task, context, format, and boundary.

00:00:55.286 --> 00:00:58.678
What detail in the story makes that goal easier to see?

00:00:59.228 --> 00:01:03.597
The tool gives a confident answer using a date Jordan does not recognize.

00:01:03.597 --> 00:01:11.017
A careful learner pauses, checks class notes, and asks where the date came from instead of building more work on top of it.

00:01:11.017 --> 00:01:12.214
Pause for a moment.

00:01:12.214 --> 00:01:15.625
What changed between the first situation and the second?

00:01:15.625 --> 00:01:19.395
If the result changed too, that is a clue worth investigating.

00:01:19.395 --> 00:01:21.430
It is not proof of the cause yet.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:26.756
Think of one more example that could help separate a genuine pattern from a coincidence.

00:01:27.306 --> 00:01:31.306
Jordan asks for one question at a time and asks the tool to wait.

00:01:31.306 --> 00:01:37.952
That small boundary changes the activity from reading another explanation into actually retrieving an idea.

00:01:37.952 --> 00:01:41.276
A useful explanation connects a detail to a decision.

00:01:41.276 --> 00:01:47.122
Try finishing this sentence in your own words: I would pay attention to this detail because...

00:01:47.122 --> 00:01:49.768
Compare a vague prompt with a focused one.

00:01:49.768 --> 00:01:53.460
You can use a different example from the one in the lesson.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.522
The goal is to explain the reasoning, not to repeat a definition.

00:01:58.072 --> 00:02:01.396
Try the prompt on a topic you already know a little.

00:02:01.396 --> 00:02:08.682
If it gives away the answer too soon, revise the instruction; if it invents detail, check a trusted class source.

00:02:08.682 --> 00:02:13.093
Before looking at any result, say what you expect to happen and why.

00:02:13.093 --> 00:02:16.736
Then compare your prediction with what actually happens.

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:22.744
If they do not match, keep the surprising result; it may be the most useful part of the test.

00:02:23.294 --> 00:02:27.325
What would you change if the first explanation were too advanced?

00:02:27.325 --> 00:02:33.588
Try asking for one everyday comparison, then check whether the comparison still matches the science.

00:02:33.588 --> 00:02:37.495
It is tempting to judge a tool by its most impressive example.

00:02:37.495 --> 00:02:44.316
A more careful check also asks who was not represented, what information is missing, and what could go wrong.

00:02:44.316 --> 00:02:47.975
Plan an attempt, feedback, and independent practice cycle.

00:02:47.975 --> 00:02:52.502
A person should be able to question a result and choose what to do next.

00:02:53.052 --> 00:02:57.485
A student can describe a math method but loses track of negative signs.

00:02:57.485 --> 00:03:04.227
Ask for a single practice problem, a hint after an attempt, and then a fresh problem to solve without help.

00:03:04.227 --> 00:03:06.225
Work through it in three moves.

00:03:06.225 --> 00:03:09.034
First, say what job the tool is meant to do.

00:03:09.034 --> 00:03:14.341
Next, point to the evidence you have, rather than a guess about how the tool thinks.

00:03:14.341 --> 00:03:17.338
Finally, decide what still needs a human check.

00:03:17.338 --> 00:03:22.020
There may be more than one reasonable answer if you explain your evidence.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:27.019
Now open the practice activity, Your prompt workshop, when you are ready.

00:03:27.019 --> 00:03:30.737
As you build a prompt, make the learner's next action clear.

00:03:30.737 --> 00:03:36.466
A request that sounds impressive is not useful if it leaves you watching instead of thinking.

00:03:36.466 --> 00:03:38.965
Take a first try before asking for help.

00:03:38.965 --> 00:03:42.865
If feedback changes your mind, name the detail that changed it.

00:03:42.865 --> 00:03:46.522
If you keep your answer, explain what evidence supports it.

00:03:47.072 --> 00:03:50.774
Your lesson project is called Plan a ten-minute study session.

00:03:50.774 --> 00:03:55.013
For your project, include a boundary that protects the student's role.

00:03:55.013 --> 00:04:00.804
The tool can ask, hint, or give feedback; decide what the student should still do independently.

00:04:00.804 --> 00:04:05.341
Before you finish, imagine someone else using your idea for the first time.

00:04:05.341 --> 00:04:07.132
What would they need to know?

00:04:07.132 --> 00:04:10.356
What could they do if the tool did not work for them?

00:04:10.356 --> 00:04:13.640
Those questions can make a promising idea more useful.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:19.273
Pick one word from today's lesson and explain it using the story you just heard.

00:04:19.273 --> 00:04:22.005
Avoid copying the wording from the screen.

00:04:22.005 --> 00:04:25.500
You might begin, In this situation, this word means...

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:29.312
If your explanation is hard to follow, add a small example.

00:04:29.312 --> 00:04:35.094
Clear language is a good way to notice what you understand and what you want to ask about.

00:04:35.644 --> 00:04:42.433
Here is one idea to take with you: The best study prompt sets up useful practice, then gives you room to do the learning.

00:04:42.433 --> 00:04:47.091
Think of a different class or school situation where the same question could help.

00:04:47.091 --> 00:04:51.299
You can write down one thing you would check and one person you could ask.

00:04:51.299 --> 00:04:55.900
That is enough for a useful next step; you do not have to solve every part today.

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:58.831
Thanks for thinking this through with me.

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:04.639
Finish the activity and project on the lesson page, then try the separate quiz when you feel ready.

00:05:04.639 --> 00:05:10.389
If a question feels difficult, return to the example and explain your reasoning in your own words.

00:05:10.389 --> 00:05:15.442
The best study prompt sets up useful practice, then gives you room to do the learning.

00:05:15.442 --> 00:05:17.707
The goal is not to trust every answer.

00:05:17.707 --> 00:05:21.482
It is to know what to check and how to make a thoughtful choice.
